.TH NETDOC 1 "2026-08-13" "network-doctor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netdoc \- diagnose network connectivity and explain where the connection breaks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netdoc
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ target ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netdoc
diagnoses network connectivity layer by layer and reports where the path
breaks in plain English. With no
.I target
it runs the generic local and internet checks; with a
.I target
it also probes that endpoint.
.PP
Probes run unprivileged and time-bounded, and form a dependency graph with
independent branches, so an unrelated failure never hides a working path.
.PP
By default
.B netdoc
starts a terminal UI. With
.B \-json
it runs headless and prints a machine-readable report instead.
.PP
Flags may be given before or after the
.IR target ,
and each accepts both the single-dash and double-dash spelling
.RB ( \-json
and
.BR \-\-json
are the same flag).
.SH TARGET
.TP
.B example.com
hostname (default port 443)
.TP
.B example.com:8022
hostname with port (protocol inferred from the port)
.TP
.B ssh://example.com:8022
URL (scheme sets protocol and default port; path ignored)
.TP
.BR 192.0.2.1 ", " 2001:db8::1
IP literal
.TP
.B [2001:db8::1]:443
IP literal with port (IPv6 needs the brackets)
.PP
Recognized URL schemes are
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
.BR ssh ,
and
.BR smtp .
At most one target may be given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-toolbox
Start in toolbox mode, which opens directly on the network tools pane
.RB ( ping ,
.BR dig ,
.BR curl ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR mtr ,
.BR ss ,
.BR ip ,
.BR nmap )
instead of the diagnosis view. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-json .
.TP
.B \-json
Run the checks headless and print a JSON report on standard output. The field
names and the status vocabulary
.RB ( PASS ,
.BR WARN ,
.BR FAIL ,
.BR SKIP ,
.BR N/A )
are a stable contract, safe to script against.
.TP
.B \-watch
Continuously re-run the checks, starting another pass five seconds after each
one finishes. In the terminal UI this shows the last 20 states and a failure
count per check. Combined with
.BR \-json ,
it streams one compact JSON report per line until the process is interrupted;
those lines carry an extra
.B ts
field (RFC 3339, UTC) and are otherwise the one-shot report unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-check " probe[,probe...]"
Run only the named stable probe IDs and the prerequisite closure derived from
the probe DAG. May be repeated; values are combined without changing canonical
probe order.
.TP
.BI \-skip " probe[,probe...]"
Omit the named stable probe IDs. Dependents whose prerequisite chain is then
unavailable are omitted too. May be repeated and combined with
.BR \-check .
Unknown or empty IDs are rejected before diagnostics start, and the error lists
every valid stable ID. A known ID that does not apply to the current target is
harmless: it selects no row with
.B \-check
and changes nothing with
.BR \-skip .
.TP
.BI \-iface " name\fR|\fPaddress"
Bind probe traffic to an interface name or to an exact local IP address, so
the checks follow a specific link (a VPN tunnel, a second NIC) rather than the
system's default route. Where the native tool supports it, the path drill-downs
follow the same selection:
.BR ping ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR mtr ,
and
.B curl
bind by interface name on Linux and macOS, or by the selected address when
.B \-\-iface
names an exact IP. Windows tools bind by source address instead. On Windows
.B ping
and
.B tracert
accept a source address for IPv6 destinations only, so an IPv4 run is unbound,
and
.B curl.exe
cannot bind by interface name, though it can use the resolved source address.
Address-only binding follows the literal target's family, then the address
selected by the target TCP check. If neither settles the family, a single-family
selection may be used, but a dual-stack selection is left unbound rather than
guessed. A missing matching source family is also left unbound. The DNS lookup
.RB ( dig ,
.B nslookup
on Windows), the
.B nmap
connect scan, and the SSH and SMTP handshake checks remain unchanged and
unbound. The DNS tools query the system resolver, often a loopback stub, rather
than the target; nmap's
.B \-S
is a spoofing option rather than documented connect-scan binding. Local-state
tools open no sockets, and the LAN scan derives its subnet from the selected
probe source address.
.TP
.BI \-public\-dns " address"
IP address of the resolver used for the second-opinion DNS check, which is
queried directly to compare its answer against the system resolver's. Must be
an IP literal, IPv4 or IPv6 \(em a hostname is rejected, since resolving it
would defeat the purpose of the check. The default is
.BR 8.8.8.8 .
An empty value
.RB ( \-\-public\-dns
.BR \(dq\(dq )
drops the check entirely: the row disappears from the report and no query
leaves the machine, which is what a strict egress policy or a privacy
requirement calls for.
.TP
.B \-no\-history
Neither read nor write the saved target history for this run, so the targets
typed in the terminal UI stay in that session only. An existing history file is
left untouched; see
.B FILES
for where it lives and how to clear it.
.TP
.BI \-keys " preset"
Keybinding preset for the terminal UI, either
.B default
or
.BR vim .
The
.B vim
preset adds
.BR gg / G
for first and last,
.BR ctrl+b / ctrl+f
for page up and down, and
.BR ctrl+u / ctrl+d
for half-page up and down inside a tool job's output; every default key keeps
working. An empty value selects
.BR default ;
any other value is rejected. There are no other presets and no keybinding
configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
Per-check probe timeout, as a Go duration string such as
.B 2s
or
.BR 1500ms .
Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The chain completed with no failed row. Skipped rows are allowed.
.TP
.B 1
A row failed, the terminal UI was quit before the chain finished, or the run
itself errored. Under
.B \-json \-watch
this is the last completed pass's status.
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments, a target the parser rejected, or no terminal on standard
output to run the terminal UI on.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run the generic local and internet diagnosis:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Diagnose the path to a host, over HTTP, TLS, and HTTPS:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Let the port select the protocol rows, here the SSH banner check:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc github.com:22
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep SSH on a nonstandard port with an explicit scheme:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc ssh://host:2222
.EE
.RE
.PP
Get one JSON report on standard output, for scripts, CI, or a bug report:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-json github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Follow an intermittent failure as it happens:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-json \-\-watch github.com | jq \-c 'select(.ok | not) | {ts, failed_stage, summary}'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Select a narrow diagnostic branch, or omit unrelated egress probes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-check dns,target_tcp,tls example.com
netdoc \-\-skip internet_tcp,quic_udp_443 example.com
netdoc \-\-check tls \-\-skip target_tcp example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send the probes out through a specific interface:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-iface wg0 github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take the second opinion from a different resolver, or drop it altogether:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc \-\-public\-dns 9.9.9.9 github.com
netdoc \-\-public\-dns "" github.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the exit status in a shell script:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc github.com || echo "path to github is broken"
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/netdoc/history
Up to 50 recent targets, saved by the terminal UI between sessions. Normally
.IR ~/.config/netdoc/history .
On macOS this is
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support/netdoc/history ,
and on Windows
.IR %AppData%\enetdoc\ehistory .
.B \-\-no\-history
skips the file entirely for one run, without removing it. Exit
.B netdoc
and delete the file to clear what is already saved.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR netdoc\-sim (1),
.BR ping (8),
.BR dig (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR traceroute (8),
.BR mtr (8),
.BR ss (8),
.BR ip (8),
.BR nmap (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Full documentation, including the JSON report schema and the verdict table:
.UR https://github.com/heymaikol/network-doctor
.UE
